Overview

The goal of this project is to model how a college network could be structured so that each department has its own isolated subnet while still being able to communicate with every other department and with a central server.

Highlights

5 × Cisco 2911 routers (1 server/core router + 4 department gateways)
5 × Cisco 2960-24TT switches (1 central + 4 department access switches)
18 PCs and 3 printers across the departments
Dedicated subnet per department, sized to the number of hosts
DHCP configured on each department router (clients get IP, gateway and DNS automatically)
Static routing between all networks, plus OSPF on the admin router
Topology
                         ┌───────────────────────┐
                         │  Router0  "server"    │
                         │  Gi0/0 10.0.0.1/24    │
                         └───────────┬───────────┘
                                     │ Fa0/1
                           ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                           │  Switch0 (central)│   10.0.0.0/24 backbone
                           └─┬───────┬───────┬─┬┘
                       Fa0/2 │ Fa0/3 │ Fa0/4 │ │ Fa0/5
                             │       │       │ │
        ┌────────────────────┘       │       │ └─────────────────────┐
        │                            │       │                       │
 ┌──────┴───────┐            ┌───────┴──────┐ ┌┴─────────────┐ ┌──────┴───────┐
 │ Router3      │            │ Router1      │ │ Router2      │ │ Router4      │
 │ "admin"      │            │ "lab"        │ │ "library"    │ │ "staf"       │
 │ 10.0.0.2     │            │ 10.0.0.3     │ │ 10.0.0.4     │ │ 10.0.0.5*    │
 │ 192.168.0.1  │            │ 192.168.1.1  │ │ 192.168.2.1  │ │ 192.168.3.1  │
 └──────┬───────┘            └───────┬──────┘ └──────┬───────┘ └──────┬───────┘
        │                            │               │                │
    Switch1                      Switch2         Switch3          Switch4
    PC0–PC2                     PC3–PC8          PC9–PC12        PC14–PC18
                                Printer1         Printer2         Printer0

* See Known Issues – the staff router is currently configured with 10.0.0.4.

Devices
Role	Device	Model	Hostname	Connected to
Server / core router	Router0	Cisco 2911	server	Switch0 (Fa0/1)
Admin gateway	Router3	Cisco 2911	admin	Switch1 (Gi0/0), Switch0 (Fa0/2)
Computer Lab gateway	Router1	Cisco 2911	lab	Switch2 (Gi0/0), Switch0 (Fa0/3)
Library gateway	Router2	Cisco 2911	library	Switch3 (Gi0/0), Switch0 (Fa0/4)
Staff gateway	Router4	Cisco 2911	staf	Switch4 (Gi0/0), Switch0 (Fa0/5)
Central switch	Switch0	Cisco 2960-24TT	–	All routers
Admin switch	Switch1	Cisco 2960-24TT	–	PC0, PC1, PC2
Lab switch	Switch2	Cisco 2960-24TT	–	PC3–PC8, Printer1
Library switch	Switch3	Cisco 2960-24TT	–	PC9–PC12, Printer2
Staff switch	Switch4	Cisco 2960-24TT	–	PC14–PC18, Printer0

Totals: 5 routers · 5 switches · 18 PCs · 3 printers

IP Addressing
Backbone (10.0.0.0/24)
Device	Interface	IP Address
Server router (Router0)	Gi0/0	10.0.0.1
Admin router (Router3)	Gi0/1	10.0.0.2
Lab router (Router1)	Gi0/1	10.0.0.3
Library router (Router2)	Gi0/1	10.0.0.4
Staff router (Router4)	Gi0/1	10.0.0.5 (intended)
Department LANs
Department	Subnet	Mask	Gateway	Hosts
Admin (principal, accounts, admission)	192.168.0.0	/29 (255.255.255.248)	192.168.0.1	3 PCs
Computer Lab	192.168.1.0	/28 (255.255.255.240)	192.168.1.1	6 PCs, 1 printer
Library	192.168.2.0	/29 (255.255.255.248)	192.168.2.1	4 PCs, 1 printer
Staff	192.168.3.0	/28 (255.255.255.240)	192.168.3.1	5 PCs, 1 printer

All end devices use DHCP, with DNS set to 8.8.8.8.

Configuration Summary
Routing: Each router has static routes to the other department networks via the 10.0.0.0/24 backbone. The server router holds routes to all four department subnets. The admin router additionally runs OSPF (process 1, area 0) on the backbone.
DHCP: Every department router runs its own DHCP pool for its LAN (default gateway = router LAN IP, DNS = 8.8.8.8).
Switching: Access switches use default VLAN 1 with PVST spanning tree.
Device access: Routers have hostnames and enable passwords configured; VTY lines require login.
<details> <summary>Static routes by router</summary>
Router	Routes
server	192.168.0.0/24 → 10.0.0.2 · 192.168.1.0/24 → 10.0.0.3 · 192.168.2.0/24 → 10.0.0.4 · 192.168.3.0/24 → 10.0.0.5
admin	192.168.1.0/24 → 10.0.0.3 · 192.168.2.0/24 → 10.0.0.4 · 192.168.3.0/24 → 10.0.0.5 + OSPF
lab	192.168.0.0/24 → 10.0.0.2 · 192.168.2.0/24 → 10.0.0.4 · 192.168.3.0/24 → 10.0.0.5
library	192.168.0.0/24 → 10.0.0.2 · 192.168.1.0/24 → 10.0.0.3 · 192.168.3.0/24 → 10.0.0.5
staff	192.168.0.0/24 → 10.0.0.2 · 192.168.1.0/24 → 10.0.0.3
</details>
How to Run
Install Cisco Packet Tracer (version 8.x or 9.x; the file was saved with 9.0).
Clone or download this repository:
bash
   git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/<repo-name>.git
Open colege-system.pkt in Packet Tracer.
Wait for the links to turn green, then click each PC → Desktop → IP Configuration → DHCP if an address has not been assigned yet.
Testing & Verification

From any PC, open Desktop → Command Prompt:

text
C:\> ipconfig                 # confirm DHCP address, gateway and DNS
C:\> ping 192.168.0.1         # admin gateway
C:\> ping 10.0.0.1            # server router
C:\> ping <PC in another dept># inter-department connectivity
C:\> tracert 192.168.2.1      # confirm the path through the backbone

On a router (CLI):

text
show ip interface brief
show ip route
show ip dhcp binding
show ip ospf neighbor         # on the admin router
Known Issues & Future Improvements

Reviewing the saved configuration turned up a few things that would make good next steps:

 Duplicate IP: The staff router's Gi0/1 is set to 10.0.0.4, the same as the library router. Every other router expects the staff router at 10.0.0.5, so it should be changed to 10.0.0.5.
 Missing route: The staff router has no route to 192.168.2.0/24 (library).
 Stray DHCP pool: The lab router has an empty pool named lam next to the working lan pool.
 Leftover switch config: Switch1 Fa0/2 has switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20 while in access mode, but no VLANs 10/20 exist.
 VLANs: Add VLANs per department/device type for better segmentation.
 Security: Add ACLs between departments, enable service password-encryption, use enable secret, and add SSH instead of plain VTY login.
 Routing: Replace the static routes with OSPF on all routers for easier scaling.
 Redundancy: Add a second link or switch to remove the single point of failure at Switch0.
 Services: Add a DNS/HTTP/FTP server and a wireless network.
Skills Demonstrated
Network design and IP subnetting (VLSM, /28 and /29 subnets)
Cisco IOS router and switch configuration
Static routing and OSPF
DHCP server configuration
Network documentation and troubleshooting
